-- YMD: the ISO 8601-ordered, BOUNDS-CHECKED date constructor.
--
-- `YMD y m d` is the recommended constructor for new code. On valid input it
-- agrees with `Date d m y` exactly (it builds through Date); on out-of-range
-- input it REFUSES, where Date silently rolls. The pair is deliberate:
-- strict literals via YMD, lenient arithmetic via Date.
--
-- Harness clock: JL4_FIXED_NOW = 2025-01-31T15:45:30Z.

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

TIMEZONE IS "Etc/UTC"


§ `YMD agrees with Date`

-- expected: DATE OF 28, 7, 2026 — big-endian arguments, little-endian display
#EVAL YMD 2026 7 28

-- expected: TRUE — same date, two argument orders
#EVAL YMD 2026 7 28 EQUALS Date 28 7 2026

-- expected: TRUE — the AKA resolves to the same constructor
#EVAL `Year month day` 2026 7 28 EQUALS YMD 2026 7 28


§ `DATE_SERIAL round-trip`

-- expected: 740189 — days since 1st January 0000
#EVAL DATE_SERIAL (YMD 2026 7 28)

-- expected: DATE OF 28, 7, 2026 — serial round-trips back to the same date
#EVAL Date (DATE_SERIAL (YMD 2026 7 28))

-- expected: TRUE
#EVAL Date (DATE_SERIAL (YMD 2026 7 28)) EQUALS YMD 2026 7 28


§ `The transposition footgun, and how each constructor answers it`

-- Transposing month and day is the mistake little-endian `Date d m y` invites,
-- and Date's failure is SILENT: month 28 is not rejected, it rolls forward.
--
-- expected: DATE OF 28, 7, 2026 — what the author meant
#EVAL YMD 2026 7 28

-- expected: DATE OF 7, 4, 2028 — `Date 7 28 2026` read as day 7, month 28,
-- year 2026; month 28 overflows 2026 by 16 months into April 2028. No error,
-- no warning, just a date two years off.
#EVAL Date 7 28 2026

-- expected: FALSE — the transposed form is a different date entirely
#EVAL Date 7 28 2026 EQUALS YMD 2026 7 28

-- expected: 740808 — 619 days adrift of the intended 740189
#EVAL DATE_SERIAL (Date 7 28 2026)

-- YMD answers the same transposition by REFUSING: the component round-trip
-- sees month 28 roll and lands on the refusal binding. A bare refused YMD
-- DISPLAYS as that binding's name (lazy WHNF); any USE of it is loud — see
-- the forcing probe below.
-- expected: `YMD refused an out-of-range month or day`
#EVAL YMD 2026 28 7


§ `Bounds: the boundary days are valid, the rolled ones refuse`

-- expected: DATE OF 31, 12, 2026 — upper boundary of the year
#EVAL YMD 2026 12 31

-- expected: DATE OF 29, 2, 2024 — a real leap day
#EVAL YMD 2024 2 29

-- expected: the refusal, by name — 2023 has no Feb 29
#EVAL YMD 2023 2 29

-- expected: DATE OF 1, 3, 2023 — Date's lenient roll of the same input,
-- unchanged and deliberate: strict literals via YMD, lenient arithmetic
-- via Date
#EVAL Date 29 2 2023

-- expected: the refusal, by name — month 0 clamps in Date; YMD refuses it
#EVAL YMD 2026 0 15

-- expected: the refusal, by name — day 32 rolls in Date; YMD refuses it
#EVAL YMD 2026 1 32

-- Forcing a refused date through arithmetic is LOUD: the machine stops with
-- "I could not continue evaluating, because I needed to know the value of
-- `YMD refused an out-of-range month or day` but it is an assumed term."
-- expected: that error
#EVAL DATE_SERIAL (YMD 2023 2 29)


§ `YMD under a rule-version axis`

-- GST rose from 7% to 9% for rules effective on/after 2024-01-01, expressed
-- with YMD throughout.
`GST rate` MEANS
  IF (DATE_SERIAL `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE`) AT LEAST (DATE_SERIAL (YMD 2024 1 1))
  THEN 9
  ELSE 7

-- expected: 7 — old rule-version
#EVAL `EVAL UNDER RULES EFFECTIVE AT` (DATE_SERIAL (YMD 2023 6 1)) `GST rate`

-- expected: 9 — new rule-version
#EVAL `EVAL UNDER RULES EFFECTIVE AT` (DATE_SERIAL (YMD 2024 7 1)) `GST rate`

-- expected: 9 — axis unset, fallback = localized today 2025-01-31
#EVAL `GST rate`
